From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:43:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: Let the idle USB-C ports power
 down

This is the J604 form of cf4eb7c2e3 ("arm64: dts: apple: t8132: Let the
idle J713 USB-C ports power down"). With the PHY and controller runtime PM
changes before it, an idle J604 USB-C port (ATC0, ATC1, ATC3) lets its
ATCn_USB domain and the ATCn_COMMON parent drop.

The SoC half of cf4eb7c2e3 is already in this tree: t8132.dtsi places the
controllers, their DARTs and the PHYs of all three ATCs in their ATCn_USB
domains, and t8132-j604-pmgr.dtsi already lets Linux manage ATCn_USB and
ATCn_COMMON (enabled, no inherited or always-on policy).

What is left is the ATCn_USB_AON parents. The J713 keeps them on, as
handed over, with apple,inherited-on. This tree's PMGR driver has no
inherited-on contract and ignores the property (the J604 policy copied
it for ATC0/ATC1 only), so once the ports idle genpd would switch the
AON parents off as well, which the J713 never does. Mark the three AON
domains apple,always-on, the policy this driver has for "stays on": they
are on today on the J604 (nothing turns them off while the ports are
runtime-active), so this keeps their state and only lets ATCn_USB and
ATCn_COMMON drop. ATC3 (USB-C port 4, right side) had neither property;
it gets the same.

apple,always-on replaces apple,inherited-on rather than joining it: a
PMGR driver with the T8132 inherited-on contract (Aurora 332eea1e90,
4d88194af4) refuses a domain that carries both. When that contract is
picked, these three should go back to apple,inherited-on alone.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604-pmgr.dtsi | 13 +++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604-pmgr.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604-pmgr.dtsi
index 33b74ca8d6..d35bfdbc1b 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604-pmgr.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604-pmgr.dtsi
@@ -15,6 +15,14 @@
  *   keeps firmware-owned, are disabled here. They are off when the J604
  *   boots and no J604 node consumes them.
  *
+ * The USB-C ports' ATCn_USB and ATCn_COMMON domains are left to runtime PM,
+ * so an idle port switches both off, as on the J713. Their ATCn_USB_AON
+ * parents stay on, as the boot loader hands them over: the J713 says so with
+ * apple,inherited-on, a contract this kernel's PMGR driver does not implement
+ * (it ignores the property), so they are apple,always-on here. The two
+ * properties must never be combined: a driver with the inherited-on contract
+ * refuses such a domain.
+ *
  * As on the J713, ps_pmc is not registered: the PMP firmware owns the PMC
  * and votes in it from its first steps (see the &pmc node). ps_gfx is
  * enabled with its gfx_tvm parent by t8132-j604-gpu.dtsi.
@@ -578,12 +586,12 @@
 
 &ps_atc0_usb_aon {
 	status = "okay";
-	apple,inherited-on;
+	apple,always-on;
 };
 
 &ps_atc1_usb_aon {
 	status = "okay";
-	apple,inherited-on;
+	apple,always-on;
 };
 
 &ps_atc0_usb {
@@ -600,6 +608,7 @@
 
 &ps_atc3_usb_aon {
 	status = "okay";
+	apple,always-on;
 };
 
 &ps_atc2_usb {
